### Tight Coupling via Direct Instantiation

Direct instantiation of concrete service classes inside a component violates three things at once:

- **The Dependency Inversion Principle (DIP)**: the component depends on concrete implementations instead of abstractions.
- **Creator**: the component is not the natural owner of these services, it only uses them, so it should not construct them.
- **Low Coupling**: every new service the component reaches for adds another hard dependency it can never swap out or mock.

Inversion of Control (IoC) resolves all three at once. Instead of the component reaching out and creating its dependencies, something external supplies them.

```typescript
// BAD: direct instantiation — DIP + Creator + Low Coupling all violated
class UserDashboard {
  private authService = new AuthService();
  private apiService = new ApiService();
  private cache = new CacheService();

  async loadUser(id: string) {
    await this.authService.validate();
    const user = await this.apiService.fetch(`/users/${id}`);
    this.cache.set(id, user);
    return user;
  }
}

// FIXED: depend on interfaces, receive implementations via a container
interface IAuthService { validate(): Promise<void>; }
interface IApiService { fetch(url: string): Promise<unknown>; }
interface ICacheService { set(key: string, value: unknown): void; }

interface ServiceContainer {
  authService: IAuthService;
  apiService: IApiService;
  cache: ICacheService;
}

class UserDashboard {
  constructor(private services: ServiceContainer) {}

  async loadUser(id: string) {
    await this.services.authService.validate();
    const user = await this.services.apiService.fetch(`/users/${id}`);
    this.services.cache.set(id, user);
    return user;
  }
}

// composition root: the ONE place concrete implementations are wired together
const container: ServiceContainer = {
  authService: new AuthService(),
  apiService: new ApiService(),
  cache: new CacheService(),
};
const dashboard = new UserDashboard(container);

// tests: swap the entire container for fakes, zero real I/O
const testDashboard = new UserDashboard({
  authService: { validate: async () => {} },
  apiService: { fetch: async () => ({ id: '1', name: 'Test' }) },
  cache: { set: () => {} },
});
```

The senior-level nuance is that IoC is not one specific tool. The same principle is implemented by all of these:

- a full dependency injection container, such as `tsyringe` or `InversifyJS`;
- a plain object passed as a constructor argument, as above;
- React Context.

In each case someone other than the consumer decides which concrete implementation to supply. That decision point is the composition root. It is pushed to the single outermost layer of the app, rather than scattered through every component that happens to need a service.

The trade-off: introducing an interface and a container for every service is real ceremony. For a component with one stable, rarely-mocked dependency, plain constructor injection without a full container is often enough. Reaching for a dependency injection framework before the number of services or the wiring complexity justifies it adds more indirection than it removes.

**Common pitfall:** Thinking IoC only means passing dependencies as arguments instead of using `new`, without also fixing the underlying abstraction.

If `ServiceContainer` held concrete classes — `AuthService` instead of `IAuthService` — the component would still be coupled to concrete implementations, just injected instead of instantiated. IoC without DIP only moves the coupling. It does not remove it.

