### Polymorphism (GRASP)

Polymorphism is one of the GRASP patterns, General Responsibility Assignment Software Patterns. It is not just use inheritance or interfaces. It is specifically about *where* varying behavior should live.

When behavior varies by type, assign responsibility for that behavior to the types themselves, through a common interface. Do not assign it to a single object that inspects each type and branches on it.

The failure mode is a central object that accumulates a growing `if/switch` on a type tag every time a new variant appears. That is the same operation, checking type over and over, in one place that has to know about every variant.

```typescript
// VIOLATION: NotificationRenderer must know every notification type
// and gets a new branch every time a variant is added
type Notification =
  | { kind: 'comment'; author: string; text: string }
  | { kind: 'like'; author: string }
  | { kind: 'mention'; author: string; context: string };

function NotificationRenderer({ n }: { n: Notification }) {
  if (n.kind === 'comment') return <p>{n.author} commented: {n.text}</p>;
  if (n.kind === 'like') return <p>{n.author} liked your post</p>;
  if (n.kind === 'mention') return <p>{n.author} mentioned you in {n.context}</p>;
  return null;
  // Adding a "follow" notification means editing this function again —
  // the responsibility for "how do I render myself" doesn't live with the data.
}

// FIXED: each notification type owns its own rendering responsibility
interface RenderableNotification {
  render(): JSX.Element;
}
class CommentNotification implements RenderableNotification {
  constructor(private author: string, private text: string) {}
  render() { return <p>{this.author} commented: {this.text}</p>; }
}
class LikeNotification implements RenderableNotification {
  constructor(private author: string) {}
  render() { return <p>{this.author} liked your post</p>; }
}
class MentionNotification implements RenderableNotification {
  constructor(private author: string, private context: string) {}
  render() { return <p>{this.author} mentioned you in {this.context}</p>; }
}

function NotificationList({ items }: { items: RenderableNotification[] }) {
  return <>{items.map((n, i) => <div key={i}>{n.render()}</div>)}</>;
}
// Adding FollowNotification means adding one new class — NotificationList
// and every existing notification class stay untouched.
```

This is Polymorphism working hand-in-hand with the Open/Closed Principle (OCP). The type-check branch is exactly the thing OCP asks you to avoid modifying. Polymorphism is the specific mechanism that achieves it: assign the behavior to the varying types.

The trade-off: turning every discriminated-union case into its own class gives up TypeScript's exhaustiveness checking. A `switch` with a `never` default catches a forgotten case at compile time, and you trade that for runtime extensibility.

For a small, closed, rarely-changing set of variants known at compile time, that `switch` can actually be the safer choice. Polymorphic dispatch has no equivalent compile-time guarantee that every case was handled.

**Common pitfall:** Reaching for a class hierarchy the moment there is more than one `if` on a type. Check first whether the set of variants is closed and stable.

Polymorphism earns its keep when new variants are added over time by different people, who should not have to touch a central function. For a fixed, small set of cases that rarely grows, a switch statement with TypeScript's exhaustiveness check is simpler. It also catches missing cases that polymorphism cannot.

